2186555717 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186555624 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555717 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555717 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555717 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186555624 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555717 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "9aa1eb7e-f2a5-549e-b007-219daa3b78ea" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555729 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186555150 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555729 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186555198 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555729 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186555589 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555729 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186555610 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555729 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186555633 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555729 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555729 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555729 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186555610 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555729 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "b92ab0a5-3040-53cf-82b8-bf078d4d8e28" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555745 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186555036 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555745 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186555143 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555745 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186555156 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555745 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186555564 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555745 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186555604 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555745 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555745 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555745 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186555156 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555745 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "d94ef671-f7de-5c89-80ee-f193344cab7d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555764 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555700 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555764 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143774" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555764 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555764 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $X$ be a continuous random variable with the following probability density function:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{1-x},\\, & x\\ge 1 \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $Y=2X,$ use the change-of-variables method to find the probability density function of $Y.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555764 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "10780b62-18db-53fc-94bd-63d320bb4c73" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555764 #edb/ref 1074 "If $X\\in (a,b)$ is a continuous random variable with probability density function ${f}_{X}$ and $Y=u(X),$ where the function $u(x)$ is strictly monotonic, then according to the change-of-variables method, the probability density function of $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}({u}^{-1}(y))\\cdot |({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)|, & y\\text{ is between }u(a)\\text{ and }u(b) \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $Y=2X.$ The transformation $u(x)=2x$ is strictly monotonically *increasing*, so the change-of-variables method can be used.\n\nFirst, we find ${u}^{-1}(y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(y) & =2y \\\\ y & =2{u}^{-1}(y) \\\\ {u}^{-1}(y) & =\\frac{y}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find $({u}^{-1})^{′}(y):$\n\n$$\n({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)=\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nAccording to the change-of-variables method, for nonzero ${f}_{Y},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={f}_{X}({u}^{-1}(y))\\cdot |({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)| \\\\  & ={f}_{X}(\\frac{y}{2})\\cdot |\\frac{1}{2}| \\\\  & ={e}^{1-y/2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{1-y/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${f}_{X}$ is nonzero on $x\\ge 1,$ the bounds are $a=1$ and $b\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nu(1)=2,\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }u(x)=\\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability distribution of $Y$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{1-y/2}, & y\\ge 2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555777 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555745 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555777 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143767" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555777 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555777 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose that $X$ is a continuous random variable. For which of the following transformations can the change-of-variables method be applied to find the probability density function of $Y?$\n\nI. $Y=\\frac{1}{2}X,$ where $X\\in [0,1]$\n\nII. $Y=\\frac{1}{{X}^{4}+1},$ where $X\\in (-2,2)$\n\nIII. $Y={X}^{2}+X,$ where $X\\in (0,\\infty )$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555777 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2b66e4d9-8056-570c-838a-8b4a8b4a119c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555777 #edb/ref 1074 "The change-of-variables method can be applied when a transformation $Y=u(X)$ is strictly increasing or strictly decreasing (i.e., strictly monotonic) over the entire support of $X.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each transformation in turn.\n\n- Transformation I is strictly monotonic. The function $u(x)=\\frac{1}{2}x$ is strictly increasing for $x\\in [0,1].$\n\n- Transformation II is *not* strictly monotonic. The function $u(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}+1}$ is strictly increasing for $x\\in (-2,0)$ and strictly decreasing for $x\\in (0,2).$\n\n- Transformation III is strictly monotonic. The function $u(x)={x}^{2}+x$ is strictly increasing for $x\\in (0,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\n.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555778 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555704 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555778 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143895" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555778 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555778 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $X$ be a continuous random variable with the following probability density function:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2-2x, & 0<x<1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $Y={X}^{2},$ use the change-of-variables method to find the probability density function of $Y.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555778 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2ba326fb-1af3-5ddd-8698-9863f73e0845" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555778 #edb/ref 1074 "If $X\\in (a,b)$ is a continuous random variable with probability density function ${f}_{X}$ and $Y=u(X),$ where the function $u(x)$ is strictly monotonic, then according to the change-of-variables method, the probability density function of $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}({u}^{-1}(y))\\cdot |({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)|, & y\\text{ is between }u(a)\\text{ and }u(b) \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $Y={X}^{2}.$ The transformation $u(x)={x}^{2}$ is strictly monotonically *increasing*, so the change-of-variables method can be used.\nFirst, we find ${u}^{-1}(y)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(y) & ={y}^{2} \\\\ y & =({u}^{-1}(y))^{2} \\\\ {u}^{-1}(y) & =\\sqrt{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find $({u}^{-1})^{′}(y):$\n\n$$\n({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}}\n$$\n\nAccording to the change-of-variables method, for nonzero ${f}_{Y},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={f}_{X}({u}^{-1}(y))\\cdot |({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)| \\\\  & =(2-2\\sqrt{y})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-\\sqrt{y}}{\\sqrt{y}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${f}_{X}$ is nonzero on $0<x<1,$ the bounds are $a=0$ and $b=1,$ we have\n\n$$\nu(0)=0,\\,u(1)=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability distribution of $Y$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1-\\sqrt{y}}{\\sqrt{y}}, & 0<y<1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555779 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555675 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555779 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143779" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555779 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555779 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose that $X$ is a continuous random variable. For which of the following transformations can the change-of-variables method be applied to find the probability density function of $Y?$\n\nI. $Y=-X,$ where $X\\in [0,1]$\n\nII. $Y=-{X}^{2},$ where $X\\in (-1,1)$\n\nIII. $Y={X}^{4},$ where $X\\in (-\\infty ,0)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555779 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2be8e1ac-e4a4-5fc5-ab2e-be1784629b6e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555779 #edb/ref 1074 "The change-of-variables method can be applied when a transformation $Y=u(X)$ is strictly increasing or strictly decreasing (i.e., strictly monotonic) over the entire support of $X.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each transformation in turn.\n\n- Transformation I is strictly monotonic. The function $u(x)=-x$ is strictly decreasing for $x\\in [0,1].$\n\n- Transformation II is *not* strictly monotonic. The function $u(x)=-{x}^{2}$ is strictly increasing for $x\\in (-1,0)$ and strictly decreasing for $x\\in (0,1).$\n\n- Transformation III is strictly monotonic. The function $u(x)={x}^{4}$ is strictly decreasing for $x\\in (-\\infty ,0).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555800 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555698 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555800 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143777" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555800 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555800 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $X$ be a continuous random variable with the following probability density function:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x}\\, & 1\\le x\\le e \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $Y=\\frac{X}{2},$ use the change-of-variables method to find the probability density function of $Y.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555800 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4cdff0a1-3127-5db2-a31d-94794e2a46b7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555800 #edb/ref 1074 "If $X\\in (a,b)$ is a continuous random variable with probability density function ${f}_{X}$ and $Y=u(X),$ where the function $u(x)$ is strictly monotonic, then according to the change-of-variables method, the probability density function of $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}({u}^{-1}(y))\\cdot |({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)|, & y\\text{ is between }u(a)\\text{ and }u(b) \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $Y=\\frac{X}{2}.$ The transformation $u(x)=\\frac{x}{2}$ is strictly monotonically *increasing*, so the change-of-variables method can be used.\n\nFirst, we find ${u}^{-1}(y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(y) & =\\frac{y}{2} \\\\ y & =\\frac{{u}^{-1}(y)}{2} \\\\ {u}^{-1}(y) & =2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find $({u}^{-1})^{′}(y):$\n\n$$\n({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)=2\n$$\n\nAccording to the change-of-variables method, for nonzero ${f}_{Y},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={f}_{X}({u}^{-1}(y))\\cdot |({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2y}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${f}_{X}$ is nonzero on $0<x<e,$ the bounds are $a=1$ and $b=e,$ we have\n\n$$\nu(1)=\\frac{1}{2},\\,u(e)=\\frac{e}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability distribution of $Y$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{y}, & \\frac{1}{2}\\le y\\le \\frac{e}{2} \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555816 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555717 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555816 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143892" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555816 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555816 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $X$ be a continuous random variable with the following probability density function:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2x, & 0<x<1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $Y=-\\sqrt{X},$ use the change-of-variables method to find the probability density function of $Y.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555816 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "674cd7aa-b875-5b4c-b5f8-f279a4fd931b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555816 #edb/ref 1074 "If $X\\in (a,b)$ is a continuous random variable with probability density function ${f}_{X}$ and $Y=u(X),$ where the function $u(x)$ is strictly monotonic, then according to the change-of-variables method, the probability density function of $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}({u}^{-1}(y))\\cdot |({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)|, & y\\text{ is between }u(a)\\text{ and }u(b) \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $Y=-\\sqrt{X}.$ The transformation $u(x)=-\\sqrt{x}$ is strictly monotonically *decreasing*, so the change-of-variables method can be used.\n\nFirst, we find ${u}^{-1}(y)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(y) & =-\\sqrt{y} \\\\ y & =-\\sqrt{{u}^{-1}(y)} \\\\ {u}^{-1}(y) & ={y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find $({u}^{-1})^{′}(y):$\n\n$$\n({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)=2y\n$$\n\nAccording to the change-of-variables method, for nonzero ${f}_{Y},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={f}_{X}({u}^{-1}(y))\\cdot |({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)| \\\\  & =2({y}^{2})\\cdot 2|y| \\\\  & =4{y}^{2}|y|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${f}_{X}$ is nonzero on $0<x<1,$ the bounds are $a=0$ and $b=1,$ we have\n\n$$\nu(0)=0,\\,u(1)=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability distribution of $Y$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-4{y}^{3}, & -1<y<0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555817 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555655 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555817 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143757" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555817 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555817 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose that $X$ is a continuous random variable. For which of the following transformations can the change-of-variables method be applied to find the probability density function of $Y?$\n\nI. $Y={X}^{5}+1,$ where $X\\in (-1,1)$\n\nII. $Y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2X,$ where $X\\in (0,\\frac{\\pi }{2})$\n\nIII. $Y=1-{X}^{4},$ where $X\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555817 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "68ad658e-fc68-5dd0-8313-20d02f0dbcab" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555817 #edb/ref 1074 "The change-of-variables method can be applied when a transformation $Y=u(X)$ is strictly increasing or strictly decreasing (i.e., strictly monotonic) over the entire support of $X.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each transformation in turn.\n\n- Transformation I is strictly monotonic. The function $u(x)={x}^{5}+1$ is strictly increasing for $x\\in (-1,1).$\n\n- Transformation II is *not* strictly monotonic. The function $u(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x$ is strictly increasing for $x\\in (0,\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ and strictly decreasing for $x\\in (\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\n- Transformation III is *not* strictly monotonic. The function $u(x)=1-{x}^{4}$ is strictly increasing for $x\\in (-\\infty ,0)$ and strictly decreasing for $x\\in (0,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555829 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555650 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555829 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143755" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555829 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555829 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose that $X$ is a continuous random variable. For which of the following transformations can the change-of-variables method be applied to find the probability density function of $Y?$\n\nI. $Y=-3X,$ where $X\\in [0,1]$\n\nII. $Y=\\frac{1}{X},$ where $X\\in (1,\\infty )$\n\nIII. $Y=\\sqrt{X},$ where $X\\in (0,\\infty )$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555829 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "83ef6537-86b1-5578-a678-ff969ac2e505" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555829 #edb/ref 1074 "The change-of-variables method can be applied when a transformation $Y=u(X)$ is strictly increasing or strictly decreasing (i.e., strictly monotonic) over the entire support of $X.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each transformation in turn.\n\n- Transformation I is strictly monotonic. The function $u(x)=-3x$ is strictly decreasing for $x\\in [0,1].$\n\n- Transformation II is strictly monotonic. The function $u(x)=\\frac{1}{x}$ is strictly decreasing for $x\\in (1,\\infty ).$\n\n- Transformation III is strictly monotonic. The function $u(x)=\\sqrt{x}$ is strictly increasing for $x\\in (0,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555851 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555663 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555851 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143775" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555851 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555851 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $X$ be a continuous random variable with the following probability density function:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{3},\\, & 0<x<1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $Y=2-X,$ use the change-of-variables method to find the probability density function of $Y.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555851 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ba6a48c1-7aec-5ddc-a819-dca70319c91f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555851 #edb/ref 1074 "If $X\\in (a,b)$ is a continuous random variable with probability density function ${f}_{X}$ and $Y=u(X),$ where the function $u(x)$ is strictly monotonic, then according to the change-of-variables method, the probability density function of $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}({u}^{-1}(y))\\cdot |({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)|, & y\\text{ is between }u(a)\\text{ and }u(b) \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $Y=2-X.$ The transformation $u(x)=2-x$ is strictly monotonically *decreasing*, so the change-of-variables method can be used.\n\nFirst, we find ${u}^{-1}(y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(y) & =2-y \\\\ y & =2-{u}^{-1}(y) \\\\ {u}^{-1}(y) & =2-y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find $({u}^{-1})^{′}(y):$\n\n$$\n({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)=-1\n$$\n\nAccording to the change-of-variables method, for nonzero ${f}_{Y},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={f}_{X}({u}^{-1}(y))\\cdot |({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)| \\\\  & ={f}_{X}(2-y)\\cdot |-1| \\\\  & =4(2-y)^{3}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4(2-y)^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${f}_{X}$ is nonzero on $0<x<1,$ the bounds are $a=0$ and $b=1,$ we have\n\n$$\nu(0)=2,\\,u(1)=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability distribution of $Y$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}4(2-y)^{3}, & 1<y<2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555855 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555729 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555855 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143773" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555855 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555855 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $X$ be a continuous random variable with the following probability density function:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}\\, & 0<x<\\pi  \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $Y=3X+3\\pi ,$ use the change-of-variables method to find the probability density function of $Y.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555855 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c6f869b2-aa11-5377-8e76-cb36213ba7a6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555855 #edb/ref 1074 "If $X\\in (a,b)$ is a continuous random variable with probability density function ${f}_{X}$ and $Y=u(X),$ where the function $u(x)$ is strictly monotonic, then according to the change-of-variables method, the probability density function of $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}({u}^{-1}(y))\\cdot |({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)|, & y\\text{ is between }u(a)\\text{ and }u(b) \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $Y=3X+3\\pi .$ The transformation $u(x)=3x+3\\pi $ is strictly monotonically *increasing*, so the change-of-variables method can be used.\n\nFirst, we find ${u}^{-1}(y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(y) & =3y+3\\pi  \\\\ y & =3{u}^{-1}(y)+3\\pi  \\\\ {u}^{-1}(y) & =\\frac{y}{3}-\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find $({u}^{-1})^{′}(y):$\n\n$$\n({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)=\\frac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nAccording to the change-of-variables method, for nonzero ${f}_{Y},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={f}_{X}({u}^{-1}(y))\\cdot |({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)| \\\\  & ={f}_{X}(\\frac{y}{3}-\\pi )\\cdot |\\frac{1}{3}| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{y}{3}-\\pi )\\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{y}{3}-\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${f}_{X}$ is nonzero on $0<x<\\pi ,$ the bounds are $a=0$ and $b=\\pi ,$ we have\n\n$$\nu(0)=3\\pi ,\\,u(\\pi )=6\\pi .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability distribution of $Y$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{y}{3}-\\pi ), & 3\\pi <y<6\\pi  \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555858 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555647 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555858 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143764" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555858 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555858 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $X$ be a continuous random variable with the following probability density function:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}},\\, & 2<x<4 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $Y=\\frac{1}{2}X-2,$ use the change-of-variables method to find the probability density function of $Y.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555858 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d414a04e-d0c3-5f7e-b95a-0d762c3601e3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555858 #edb/ref 1074 "If $X\\in (a,b)$ is a continuous random variable with probability density function ${f}_{X}$ and $Y=u(X),$ where the function $u(x)$ is strictly monotonic, then according to the change-of-variables method, the probability density function of $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}({u}^{-1}(y))\\cdot |({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)|, & y\\text{ is between }u(a)\\text{ and }u(b) \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $Y=\\frac{1}{2}X-2.$ The transformation $u(x)=\\frac{1}{2}x-2$ is strictly monotonically *increasing*, so the change-of-variables method can be used.\n\nFirst, we find ${u}^{-1}(y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(y) & =\\frac{1}{2}y-2 \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{-1}(y)-2 \\\\ {u}^{-1}(y) & =2y+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find $({u}^{-1})^{′}(y):$\n\n$$\n({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)=2\n$$\n\nAccording to the change-of-variables method, for nonzero ${f}_{Y},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={f}_{X}({u}^{-1}(y))\\cdot |({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)| \\\\  & ={f}_{X}(2y+4)\\cdot |2| \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{(2y+4)^{2}}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{(y+2)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${f}_{X}$ is nonzero on $2<x<4,$ the bounds are $a=2$ and $b=4,$ we have\n\n$$\nu(2)=-1,\\,u(4)=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability distribution of $Y$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{(y+2)^{2}}, & -1<y<0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555866 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555708 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555866 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143756" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555866 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555866 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose that $X$ is a continuous random variable. For which of the following transformations can the change-of-variables method be applied to find the probability density function of $Y?$\n\nI. $Y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡X,$ where $X\\in [1,\\infty )$\n\nII. $Y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡X,$ where $X\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi )$\n\nIII. $Y={X}^{3},$ where $X\\in (-10,10)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555866 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e6df473a-21d5-53f9-84c7-d9c14b1c57fc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555866 #edb/ref 1074 "The change-of-variables method can be applied when a transformation $Y=u(X)$ is strictly increasing or strictly decreasing (i.e., strictly monotonic) over the entire support of $X.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each transformation in turn.\n\n- Transformation I is strictly monotonic. The function $u(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is strictly increasing for $x\\in [1,\\infty ).$\n\n- Transformation II is *not* strictly monotonic. The function $u(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ is strictly increasing for $x\\in (-\\pi ,0)$ and strictly decreasing for $x\\in (0,\\pi ).$\n\n- Transformation III is strictly monotonic. The function $u(x)={x}^{3}$ is strictly increasing for $x\\in (-10,10).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555873 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555652 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555873 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143894" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555873 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555873 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $X$ be a continuous random variable with the following probability density function:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{3}}{4}, & 0<x<2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $Y=\\sqrt[3]{X},$ use the change-of-variables method to find the probability density function of $Y.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555873 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ff19e38b-ccbe-5836-a668-3a89307b9227" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555873 #edb/ref 1074 "If $X\\in (a,b)$ is a continuous random variable with probability density function ${f}_{X}$ and $Y=u(X),$ where the function $u(x)$ is strictly monotonic, then according to the change-of-variables method, the probability density function of $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}({u}^{-1}(y))\\cdot |({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)|, & y\\text{ is between }u(a)\\text{ and }u(b) \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $Y=\\sqrt[3]{X}.$ The transformation $u(x)=\\sqrt[3]{x}$ is strictly monotonically *increasing*, so the change-of-variables method can be used.\n\nFirst, we find ${u}^{-1}(y)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(y) & =\\sqrt[3]{y} \\\\ y & =\\sqrt[3]{{u}^{-1}(y)} \\\\ {u}^{-1}(y) & ={y}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find $({u}^{-1})^{′}(y):$\n\n$$\n({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)=3{y}^{2}\n$$\n\nAccording to the change-of-variables method, for nonzero ${f}_{Y},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={f}_{X}({u}^{-1}(y))\\cdot |({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)| \\\\  & ={f}_{X}({y}^{3})\\cdot |3{y}^{2}| \\\\  & =\\frac{{({y}^{3})}^{3}}{4}\\cdot 3{y}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{y}^{9}}{4}\\cdot 3{y}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{y}^{11}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${f}_{X}$ is nonzero on $0<x<2,$ the bounds are $a=0$ and $b=2,$ we have\n\n$$\nu(0)=0,\\,u(2)=\\sqrt[3]{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability distribution of $Y$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3{y}^{11}}{4}, & 0<y<\\sqrt[3]{2} \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
